# Line endings, pinned where a carriage return breaks a file instead of merely looking odd.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine git setting, so without this file the bytes your clone receives
# depend on how your git is configured rather than on what this repository stores. Every path below
# is stored here with LF; on a machine where `core.autocrlf` is enabled (the default of git on
# Windows) a checkout rewrites them to CRLF, and the joining kit stops working before it does
# anything:
#
#     $ bash deploy/hw_probe.sh
#     set: usage: set [-abefhkmnptuvxBCEHPT] ...
#     deploy/hw_probe.sh: line 20: $'\r': command not found
#     deploy/hw_probe.sh: line 22: syntax error near unexpected token `$'in\r''
#
# The carriage return is invisible in an editor, the file looks perfect, and the error names a line
# that has nothing wrong with it. This applies to `bash script.sh` as much as to `./script.sh`: the
# shell is parsing the bytes either way, so the shebang is not what saves you.
#
# The first group below is what a POSIX shell, `make`, a container runtime or a shell-read config file
# consumes, where a carriage return breaks the file. Python, Markdown, YAML, JSON and Go are pinned
# further down for a different reason: there a carriage return breaks the diff, not the file.
#
# Check your own clone with:  git ls-files --eol '*.sh' | grep -v 'i/lf'   (empty = nothing to fix)

# Shell scripts: the joining kit, the node/miner/validator helpers, the container entrypoints.
*.sh                    text eol=lf

# Read line by line by tools that do not strip a carriage return.
Makefile                text eol=lf
Dockerfile*             text eol=lf
docker/Caddyfile        text eol=lf

# Read by a shell and compared as a string: a trailing carriage return makes an equal value unequal,
# and that failure leaves no error message at all.
docker/CONSENSUS_EPOCH  text eol=lf
docker/GENESIS_SHA256   text eol=lf
docker/KIT_VERSION      text eol=lf
docker/MINER_IMAGE      text eol=lf
docker/NODE_IMAGE       text eol=lf
docker/STATESYNC_RPC    text eol=lf
docker/CLOUD_IMAGE      text eol=lf

# The HiveOS package: HiveOS sources h-manifest.conf and runs the h-* scripts with bash, and
# deploy/hiveos/build.sh refuses a carriage return in any of them, so a Windows clone must not add one.
deploy/hiveos/**        text eol=lf

# Prose and Python are pinned for the diff: a carriage return does not break them (CPython reads CRLF,
# Markdown does not care), but a contributor on Linux who edits one CRLF file sends back a whole-file
# change, and the review is unreadable. The rule normalises at check-in, so nobody gets a clone that
# looks modified before they touch it.
*.md                    text eol=lf
*.py                    text eol=lf
*.yml                   text eol=lf
*.json                  text eol=lf
# The chain itself: every Go source and proto definition. Unpinned, a clone on a machine whose
# `core.autocrlf` is true receives them with carriage returns, and two gits reading such a tree then
# disagree about what is modified; a build seal was once stamped `-dirty` on a clean commit this way.
*.go                    text eol=lf
*.proto                 text eol=lf

# Never end-of-line converted, never diffed as text.
*.png                   binary
*.ico                   binary
*.svg                   text
